From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josef Ridky <jridky@redhat.com>
Date: Mon, 02 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fix uninitialised memory in client control mode

In client_main(), the struct termios tio is used uninitialised.
cfmakeraw() applies bitwise operations (&= / |=) to the existing
field values, so garbage from the stack leaks through into c_lflag,
c_cc[], and c_line. On Linux, where NOKERNINFO is not defined,
c_lflag is never explicitly assigned, so it retains whatever bits
cfmakeraw() left from the original garbage. A random c_line value
can switch the pty line discipline, and random c_cc[] entries can
alter how the kernel tty layer processes bytes, both of which can
cause data loss on the SSH pty in control mode.

Fix by copying saved_tio into tio before modifying it, so every
field starts from a known-good state.

Also in client_stdin_callback(), struct msg_stdin_data is not zeroed
before use. When read() returns fewer bytes than sizeof data.data,
the remainder is uninitialised stack memory which is then sent over
the Unix socket via proc_send(). Fix by zeroing the struct before
the read.
---
 client.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/client.c b/client.c
index 1234567..abcdefg 100644
--- a/client.c
+++ b/client.c
@@ -315,6 +315,7 @@ client_main(struct event_base *base, int argc, char **argv, int flags)
 			    strerror(errno));
 			return (1);
 		}
+		memcpy(&tio, &saved_tio, sizeof tio);
 		cfmakeraw(&tio);
 		tio.c_iflag = ICRNL|IXANY;
 		tio.c_oflag = OPOST|ONLCR;
@@ -438,6 +439,7 @@ client_stdin_callback(__unused int fd, __unused short events,
 {
 	struct msg_stdin_data	data;

+	memset(&data, 0, sizeof data);
 	data.size = read(STDIN_FILENO, data.data, sizeof data.data);
 	if (data.size < 0 && (errno == EINTR || errno == EAGAIN))
 		return;
